description:
Site: crate-jmap-chat-server/src/message.rs:669-688 (forbidden-reaction check).

Code:
    for (_, suffix) in &decoded {
        if let Some(existing) = msg.reactions.get(suffix) {
            if existing.sender_id != SenderId::Owner {
                bad.push(suffix.clone());
            }
        }
    }

Spec context (draft-atwood-jmap-chat-00 §Reaction): only the original sender of a reaction may modify it. The handler's intent is to enforce that.

Problem: SenderId::Owner is a tag meaning 'the account that owns this mailbox', not 'this specific caller'. In any deployment with:
- shared mailboxes (multiple users authenticating to the same account)
- delegated access (admin/assistant access)
- multi-user JMAP accounts

…the check 'existing.sender_id != SenderId::Owner' returns false for ANY reaction tagged Owner, regardless of which user actually authored it. That lets user A modify user B's reactions whenever both share an account.

The correct check requires caller-identity comparison: a reaction has a specific authoring principal (or a 'self' marker resolved against B::principal_id(caller)). The kit's foundation seam provides principal_id; the type system stores SenderId::Owner|Peer (no principal id discriminator).

Two fix paths:
1. Extend Reaction to carry an authoring principal id at storage time; compare on read.
2. Resolve principal_id at modify time, and the backend per-reaction maintains an authoring map; if absent, reject (fail closed).

Either way, the current implementation collapses 'original sender' to 'this account' and breaks the spec rule in multi-user deployments.

Single-user-mode tests pass because the check trivially holds; the bug is invisible until a real identity backend is wired.
